  "response": "## Finding\n\n`crime_00041` — **Oversized Raster** (\"shrink_raster\"), `src/assets/hero-banner.png`\n\n> **byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)**\n> severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB\n> format: png\n\nSeverity `low` (0.4), confidence `0.95`, effort `quick`, agent_risk 0.04.\n\n## Verdict: request changes — do not block\n\n254 KB sits on the *lowest* rung of the detector's own ladder, barely over the 200 KB floor and nowhere near the 500 KB / 1 MB rungs. Confidence is 0.95 because it's a byte count, not an inference — the finding is definitely true, it's just not severe. Nothing is broken; the page ships and works.\n\nBut this is the **hero**, which makes it almost certainly the LCP element on the landing page, so it's the one image on the site where 254 KB is paid on the critical path by every first-time visitor. Combined with `effort: quick` and `risk: low`, there's no good argument for merging it as-is and filing a follow-up — the fix is a re-export, and it's cheaper to do in this PR than to track.\n\nSo: **request changes, merge on the re-encode.** Block only if you have a hard page-weight budget in CI that this breaks.\n\n## Recommended transformation\n\n**One caveat first:** the finding reports bytes and format only — it does **not** report pixel dimensions for this file (contrast `crime_00040`, where the detector did emit `32 × 32 px`). So the resize target can't be read off the scan; it has to come from the CSS render box. Measure the rendered `<img>`/background at your largest breakpoint before picking numbers.\n\n**Dimensions.** Assuming a conventional full-bleed hero capped at a ~1280–1440 px content container:\n\n| Variant | Intrinsic px | Serves |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hero-banner-800` | 800 × H | mobile 1×, small 2× |\n| `hero-banner-1600` | 1600 × H | desktop 1×, mobile 2× |\n| `hero-banner-2400` | 2400 × H | desktop 2× / retina cap |\n\nCap at 2400 px wide. Beyond ~2× the largest CSS width the extra pixels are indistinguishable and cost real bytes. If the current PNG is a 3840 px or 4K export dropped in unresized — which 254 KB of PNG strongly suggests — that alone is most of the win. Wire it up with `srcset` + `sizes` rather than shipping one file to everyone.\n\nTarget **≤100 KB for the largest variant**, realistically 40–80 KB.\n\n**Format: AVIF, with a WebP fallback.**\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source srcset=\"hero-banner-800.avif 800w, hero-banner-1600.avif 1600w, hero-banner-2400.avif 2400w\"\n          sizes=\"100vw\" type=\"image/avif\">\n  <source srcset=\"hero-banner-800.webp 800w, hero-banner-1600.webp 1600w, hero-banner-2400.webp 2400w\"\n          sizes=\"100vw\" type=\"image/webp\">\n  <img src=\"hero-banner-1600.webp\" width=\"1600\" height=\"600\"\n       fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"…\">\n</picture>\n```\n\nWhy AVIF first, specifically here:\n\n- **It's photographic and it's the LCP element.** AVIF typically lands 30–50% under WebP at matched perceptual quality on photographic content. On a hero — the largest single asset on the page, on the critical path — that delta is worth having; on a 4 KB icon it wouldn't be.\n- **Support is no longer the tiebreaker.** AVIF is in Chrome 85+, Firefox 93+, and Safari 16+. The `<picture>` fallback covers the tail for free, so choosing AVIF costs nothing in reach.\n- **PNG is the actual mistake.** PNG is lossless — it's paying full price to preserve detail no visitor can perceive in a hero photograph. Both AVIF and WebP support alpha, so transparency isn't a reason to stay on PNG either.\n\n**When I'd pick WebP instead:** if the hero is flat vector-ish artwork — hard edges, large solid fields, rendered text — AVIF's advantage narrows sharply, and at aggressive quality settings its edge handling smears text in a way WebP doesn't. AVIF also encodes slowly (a build-time cost) and decodes heavier than WebP, which counts against it precisely because this is the LCP image. For a flat graphic hero: single WebP, skip the AVIF tier, keep the pipeline simple.\n\nEncode at `cq-level 28–32` (AVIF) / `quality 78–82` (WebP) and check the result by eye — hero images are the one place where trusting the quality slider blind is a bad idea.\n\n## Two adjacent notes for the same PR\n\nThe asset directory has other findings worth folding in while someone is already in there:\n\n- `crime_00038` (**high**, conf 0.95) — `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` has 2 base64 rasters (JPEG + PNG) embedded in it. Higher severity than the hero, same directory, same class of problem.\n- `crime_00040` (low) — `src/assets/check-icon.png` is a 32×32 PNG that should be an SVG.\n\nNeither is this PR's fault, so neither belongs in the merge gate for it — but if the team is doing an asset pass, `crime_00038` is the one with the real severity score.\n\nWorth adding a per-image size budget to CI so the next hero doesn't need a human to notice.",
